A||||21||BOILERMAKER||Rear End Dancing
Z||000000||BOILERMAKER||05-11-99||07:17 AM||||I have an 87 SE with the honeycomb wheels on it.  I have a problem with the back end dancing and not finding a centerline when torque is applied (gas pedal pushed down ... although not all the way).  I have had this beast aligned twice and from the print-outs, everything is within spec.  <p>I get going down the road and when I start to accelerate, my back-end seems not to be able to find a center-line.  It shifts from one side to the other and then when I decelerate, it shifts back to where it was before.  Of course, if I find it's center-of-torque (where the shifting begins)the back-end dances all over the road.  <p>Any suggestions???????????  Is there a different set of specs that I should be going by for the alignment?????  I have checked all suspension and everything is satisfactory.  Nothing moves!  I am quite stumped.  I have also checked cradle mounts and nothing moves.<p><br>Thanks in advance
Z||000001||theogre||05-11-99||08:21 AM||||your rear is moving under load. you will find this extreamly dificult to reproduce at rest.<p>The problem is commonly caused by the rear spindles, the controll arm bushings, or the cradel bushings. one or all of these need replacing.<p>Replacing all the bushings in the suspension & cradle with urethane is the prefered fix. Replace the front and rear suspension bushings as a complete project or you could have some very dangerous handling conditions. Do Not mix rubber and urethane bushings. You can replace the cradle/body mounts as a seperate project without causing a problem.<p>any damage to the spindles means replacement of them. If you aren't sure of the spindles and how to check them out then find someone that can. guessing with these can cause a nasty mess.
Z||000002||BOILERMAKER||05-11-99||10:28 AM||||Wouldn't any of these problems cause abnormal movement somewhere in either the cradle or the suspension????  I have prodded and pried (not excessively) and have produced no abnormal movement.<p>I understand what your comments are, but couldn't you say the same thing about the struts?  If the struts were worn and shot, this could cause some nasty movement also, but I know that they are fine and the prying and prodding that I have done would show this.<p>I appreciate the comments and welcome more, but I have to find out as much as I can so I can get my baby back on the road.<p>Thanks
Z||000003||theogre||05-11-99||11:46 AM||||you would think you could see it, but in fiero's case, you often can't see this at rest. The problem isn't usually one bad bushing. It's usually a summing effect of a group of marginal ones. Individual areas seem OK, but when the engine loads up the whole system things start moving. The small movments of each of the parts sum up to some fairly unpredictable totals.<p>you've got to remember that all the original parts on the car are over a decade old. Ozone, oil, and other contaminates have been eating away at those rubbers for a long time. They simply are not as stiff as they were new, no mater how good they look or feel.<p>Even when new Fiero rears were known for being a bit flaky. Any sudden change from power to breaking, by either hitting the breaks, or sudden throtle closure, Will shift the rear end alignment. Marginal bushings make this effect even worse. This is why we all talk so much about urethane, and other, bushing setups here in the forums. 
Z||000004||PaUL||05-11-99||02:21 PM||||Ive seen these symtoms you describe before. I thinks its either your front engine mount bolts or your struts are worn. Check the 2 large bolts holding the alternater bracket and mounting plate to the block. <p> 
Z||000005||BOILERMAKER||05-17-99||07:20 AM||||I guess we will see what has my little baby under the weather.  She went in for a closer look at the not-so-local shop (the only one that I trust ... that's a whole other topic in itself).
Z||000006||BOILERMAKER||05-27-99||09:38 AM||||I know everyone has gotten burnt by crooked mechanics, but keep an eye out.  Seems as if more and more are popping up.  I just got an answer back on my 87.  They said that it was the half-shafts and joints that could be wrong.  they said that they weren't for certain, but that would be the next step.<p>They didn't even put it back on the rack to check the alignment again to see if anything was moving.<p>Great people.  You know ... it seems to be the same people all of the time.  The ones who want to get you out of their garage and get somebody else in ASAP.
Z||000007||Racer 1||06-02-99||12:08 PM||||Hey your shocks are worn out in back mine did the same thing when side 2 side motion in back!I replaced them and everthing is fine now!!!!! 
Z||000008||BOILERMAKER||06-02-99||02:23 PM||||I appreciate your input into my dilemna, but I know for a fact that both my shocks and struts are new and not shot.
Z||000009||theogre||06-02-99||02:48 PM||||I doubt a half shaft would cause that. I've had halfshafts binding badly as they failed and they just don't work like that.<p>It could be the rear spindle assembly, but that's fairly easy to check with a dial gauge. If those are bad it's not hard to tell.<p>Other wise, I already told you some of what to look at above. I recently drove an 84 that's exactly like you describe. Every time you hit the gas or the brake the thing steers itselfe. It's also got known good shocks/struts and a recent alignment. the rear bushings are simply gone from age. It's time consuming, but not rocket science to fix them. You will need another alignment when you are done.
Z||000010||lowCG||06-02-99||11:16 PM||||Hi,went on a year long sabatical(see forum topic"suspension geometry"-JHansen)that actually started with the wandering;replaced the rear control arm bushings with solid bearings,and the problem remained.Quite puzzled,I looked on to the front end, where I found that the(lower)ball joints were sloppy,you can test this part with a very large pair of Channel-Lock pliers by squeezing very hard,the joints two(upper/lower)parts;if there is any felt movement,they're toast.After replacing these,all was fine,for the moment anyhow...
Z||000011||Carrolles||06-03-99||08:00 AM||||A friend of mine has an 85SE like mine and had similar problems.  It turned out to be shot front ball joints.
Z||000012||BOILERMAKER||06-03-99||08:16 AM||||Well, ball joints were checked last month or so and I know for a fact that these are not the problem.  I only wish it were that simple.
Z||000013||Dan||06-16-99||09:40 AM||||Hey, keep me posted. I have an 85 2m4. My problem isn't throttle or brakes, but when I reach about 70 mph and do a lane change. When I bring the wheel to center the car goes wobbley. Pretty scary. I have all new front end bushings, alignment, and getting ready to replace rear bushings. I'll let you know how I come out. Meanwhile if anyone had similar problems let me know. 
Z||000014||Eric||06-16-99||01:48 PM||||Did you check the ball joints, Dan?  Mine lowers were worn when I first got the car and I experienced the same problems.  
Z||000015||lowCG||06-16-99||04:57 PM||||Transverse motors,in the rear,will load and unload the front end with torque application,and reveal any slop in the lower ball joints in doing so.<br>Unfortunately,the replacement(TRW,dealer sourced)ball joints only lasted me 3 months before I took them out again,saw that they were junk by design/metal quality,and replaced them with '71-'82 Cadillac Coupe De Ville upper units,welded to the control arm.<br>I had to add an extra thick washer under the nut,re-allign the car again too.
Z||000016||PaUL||06-16-99||06:40 PM||||3 months?!? lowCG, you must drive like a madman. Anyway I just wanted to tell everyone that if their front lower balljoints are worn, replace them immediately. I had one pop out of its socket while I was driving. This is described in a earlier post called <b>Bad Balls Bad Experience</b>.
Z||000017||lowCG||06-16-99||09:41 PM||||Well Paul,I was beginning to think so too,at least until I returned the parts,and found that the NEW,unused parts were loose where the ball is(supposed to be)swedged onto the stud!So they were most likely no good from the beginning,I just didn't check them out the first time.<br>The parts weren't totally shot,by the apparently normal,wait-till-it-smacks-you-inna-head standards,however,I could tell,by being one who actually worked on my own car,just weeks before,that they were sloppy again.<br>Oh well,maybe I'm just picky.<p><br> 
Z||000018||Cooter||06-17-99||09:26 PM||||On the TRW suspension parts, I had the same problem- they were junk from the first day. Switched over to Moog chasis parts and never had another problem. Federal mogul makes trw stuff, and most federal mogul stuff is low quality. 
Z||000019||lowCG||06-17-99||10:49 PM||||Thanks for the lowdown! Now I won't have to(painfully)search for German Opel uppers for a reasonable replacement.Will continue with using(Moog)Caddy ones for the lowers,as they allow the upper control arm to raise up a bit and reduce bumpsteer(good)as a result.
Z||000020||Dan||07-01-99||11:19 AM||||Wasn't ball joints, just bad tire.<br>
Z||000021||theogre||07-01-99||10:09 PM||||Tire's will do that.......... teach us all to skip the obvious.
